#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059375 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "979859e4-b60c-548d-8da6-b180400e1b07" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "14e05d4f-373c-5d1f-aa59-7ee52466480e" :question/math-academy-id "e-14762" :question/problem "Find the value of $\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 & 6 \\\\ 8 & 8 & 8 \\\\ -6 & -12 & 1\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 & 6 \\\\ 8 & 8 & 8 \\\\ -6 & -12 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =0|\\begin{aligned}8 & 8 \\\\ -12 & 1\\end{aligned}|-(-3)|\\begin{aligned}8 & 8 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{aligned}|+6|\\begin{aligned}8 & 8 \\\\ -6 & -12\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0(8+96)+3(8+48)+6(-96+48) \\\\  & =0+3\\cdot 56+6\\cdot (-48) \\\\  & =168-288 \\\\  & =-120\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059376 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "32df8909-a239-5167-b8ea-412f7cdaea93" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Singular Matrices"}] :question/id #uuid "d7516cec-bd96-59db-98cd-5d82e41fa719" :question/math-academy-id "e-14763" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $A$ given below?\n\n- $\\det(A) = 0$\n\n- $A$ is **not** a singular matrix\n\n- $A$ is a singular matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 7\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 7\\end{aligned}|-2|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 7\\end{aligned}|+3|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(0-4)-2(7-3)+3(4-0) \\\\  & =-4-2\\cdot 4+3\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-4-8+12 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\det(A)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements I and III are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059377 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab642ad6-d9e0-5734-83b4-81627b9132eb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Given the Determinant"}] :question/id #uuid "7780340c-43d6-5d38-9270-865914098218" :question/math-academy-id "e-14764" :question/problem "Given that the determinant of  $[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ k & 4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ is $2,$ find the value of the constant $k.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $k\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ k & 4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}| & =3|\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|-1|\\begin{aligned}k & 5 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}|+2|\\begin{aligned}k & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3(12-10)-1(3k-0)+2(2k-0) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 3k+2\\cdot 2k \\\\  & =6-3k+4k \\\\  & =k+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the determinant of the matrix is $2,$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k+6 & =2 \\\\ k & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277588 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "32df8909-a239-5167-b8ea-412f7cdaea93" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Singular Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7b52202-5838-5f29-a879-c2b165ec65a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277590} {:answer/id #uuid "2c96c50d-442d-5f35-9ce3-641ad01fa89d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277591} {:answer/id #uuid "d830578e-b1ce-5019-9827-ef1d027857fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277592} {:answer/id #uuid "31226bb1-7aa2-5b8d-b4d2-e4f06b081910" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277593} {:answer/id #uuid "934fefcf-c3c7-5069-a704-8f6172567660" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277594}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31226bb1-7aa2-5b8d-b4d2-e4f06b081910" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277593} :answer-field/id #uuid "84cbf4eb-9c25-5589-8b2a-668a1583cc9e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277589}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "72bad0dd-a3a7-570e-b5f6-c6a202014e4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-128484" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $R$ given below?\n\n- $R$ is a singular matrix\n- $det(R)=0$\n- $det(R)=1$\n\n \n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}2 & \\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & \\sqrt{2} & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(R) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & \\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & \\sqrt{2} & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2|\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & 4\\end{aligned}|-\\sqrt{2}|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & -1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}|+2|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 1 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(0+\\sqrt{2})-\\sqrt{2}(2+1)+2(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-0) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 3+2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}-3\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $det(R)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277616 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "979859e4-b60c-548d-8da6-b180400e1b07" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e23f9df-fd1c-5fb0-94ae-f6375cd3d955" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-146" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277618}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e23f9df-fd1c-5fb0-94ae-f6375cd3d955" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-146" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277618} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab1047e4-3763-5e81-9313-32ad69e87013" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277617}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ab606913-f467-54bc-b8f4-c2c664d43234" :question/math-academy-id "q-280387" :question/problem "If $H=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}],$ then $det(H)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}0 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}| & =0|\\begin{aligned}0 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}|-7|\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}|+2|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0-7(0-(-20))+2(-3-0) \\\\  & =-7\\cdot (20)+2\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =-140-6 \\\\  & =-146\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277622 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "979859e4-b60c-548d-8da6-b180400e1b07" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc5a18cf-619b-5487-8d6e-7bb9706e4c13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277624}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc5a18cf-619b-5487-8d6e-7bb9706e4c13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277624} :answer-field/id #uuid "5aa8dc84-e4fd-5d51-b298-97c10fc7b514" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277623}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e83248f9-81a1-5e37-b8ca-6d95511aa92e" :question/math-academy-id "q-280394" :question/problem "If $R=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 6 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}],$ then $det(R)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 6 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & =1|\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|-(-2)|\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ 6 & 2\\end{aligned}|+0|\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 6 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(3\\cdot 2-5\\cdot (-1))+2(4\\cdot 2-5\\cdot 6)+0 \\\\  & =1(6+5)+2(8-30) \\\\  & =11+2(-22) \\\\  & =11-44 \\\\  & =-33\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
